  "tree_id": 1484,
  "family": "Fagaceae",
  "memo": "Although popular in cultivation, the Island Oak is the rarest of California's oaks in the wild, endemic to only five California islands and Guadalupe Island off the west coast of Baja California. It is listed as an endangered species by the IUCN, threatened by overgrazing from feral goats and sheep. This smooth-barked, medium-sized tree forms a rounded, dark green canopy. Its leathery, evergreen leaves have pleated parallel side veins, and are shiny on the upper surface while being covered in woolly hairs underneath. It forms hybrids easily with &lt;em&gt;Q. chrysolepis&lt;/em&gt;. In cultivation, this species is fast-growing and low-maintenance.&nbsp;",
